---
/**
 * What to do when macOS refuses to open the downloaded app.
 *
 * This page exists because the refusal does not read like a policy decision.
 * Gatekeeper's wording for an app it cannot verify is "damaged and can't be
 * opened" — which describes a corrupt download, not an unsigned one. Without
 * a page to land on, the honest reading of that message is "the file is
 * broken", and the download is thrown away.
 *
 * The site's download buttons lead to the command-line install, which macOS
 * does not block (2026-10-02). This page serves visitors who take the disk
 * image from the GitHub release page instead; the code signing policy links
 * to it.
 *
 * TEMPORARY. Once the Developer ID notarization in the app repo's
 * `.github/workflows/macos-release.yml` is live, the first launch is an
 * ordinary double-click and this page should be deleted, along with
 * `MACOS_FIRST_LAUNCH_HREF` in `src/lib/install.ts`.
 */
import LegalPage from "@/components/LegalPage.astro";
import { CODE_SIGNING_HREF } from "@/lib/legal";
import { REPO } from "@/lib/install";
---

<LegalPage
  title="Opening Personal Jarvis the first time on macOS — Personal Jarvis"
  description="macOS blocks the Personal Jarvis disk image on first launch because it is not yet notarized by Apple. Here is what the warning means and how to open the app."
  heading="Opening Personal Jarvis the first time on macOS"
>
  <p>
    The first time you open Personal Jarvis on a Mac, macOS may refuse it and
    say the app <strong>&ldquo;is damaged and can&rsquo;t be opened&rdquo;</strong>,
    or that it <strong>cannot be checked for malicious software</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The download is not damaged. That message is what macOS shows for any app
    that has not been through Apple&rsquo;s notarization service, and Personal
    Jarvis has not — notarization requires a paid Apple Developer membership,
    which this project does not hold yet. The wording is Apple&rsquo;s, not
    ours, and it is the same for every unnotarized app. Our
    <a href={CODE_SIGNING_HREF}>code signing policy</a> tracks where this
    stands.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can verify the file you downloaded against the checksums published
    with each release on the
    <a href={`${REPO}/releases/latest`}>release page</a>, and the app is built
    in public from the source in
    <a href={REPO}>this repository</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Opening it on macOS 15 Sequoia or newer</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Drag Personal Jarvis into your Applications folder, then double-click it
      once and dismiss the warning. <strong>Do not skip this step.</strong>
      macOS only offers the override below for an app you have actually tried
      to open, so on a fresh install the button in step 3 is not there yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      Open <strong>System Settings &rsaquo; Privacy &amp; Security</strong> and
      scroll to the <strong>Security</strong> section near the bottom.
    </li>
    <li>
      You will see a line naming Personal Jarvis as blocked, with an
      <strong>Open Anyway</strong> button next to it. Click it and authenticate.
    </li>
    <li>Open the app again and confirm once more. From then on it opens normally.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="legal-doc__note">
    On macOS 14 Sonoma and earlier you can instead right-click the app and
    choose <strong>Open</strong>, which offers the override directly. Apple
    removed that shortcut in Sequoia, so on current systems use the steps above.
  </p>

  <h2>Or from the terminal</h2>
  <p>
    If you prefer one command, this removes the quarantine flag that your
    browser attached to the download:
  </p>
  <pre><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "/Applications/Personal Jarvis.app"</code></pre>
  <p class="legal-doc__note">
    Run this only on software you meant to install and obtained from a source
    you trust. It switches off a safety check, so it is worth understanding
    rather than pasting by habit — here it clears the flag your browser sets on
    every download, which is what makes macOS treat the app as unverified.
  </p>

  <h2>When this goes away</h2>
  <p>
    Once the project holds an Apple Developer membership, releases will be
    signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple. The first launch then
    becomes an ordinary double-click with no warning and no extra steps, and
    this page is retired.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
